// Psychology Units 3 & 4 – Practice Exam 3 (VCAA format). All scenarios and questions are original.
// Correct options are authored first; VCE.finalize() rebalances answer positions at load.

VCE.stimuli('psychology', {
  'e3-swim': { html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 1–3.</em></p><p>Ruby is swimming in cold ocean water. Temperature receptors in her skin detect the cold, goosebumps appear on her arms and her heart rate rises. After a few minutes she decides she has had enough and swims back to shore.</p>` },
  'e3-rats': {
    html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 4–6.</em></p><p>Rats were placed in a box in which pressing a lever delivered a sugar pellet. Twenty rats were randomly allocated to two groups. Before each daily session, Group 1 was injected with a drug that blocks dopamine receptors; Group 2 was injected with a salt solution that has no effect. The mean number of lever presses per 30-minute session was recorded over five days.</p>`,
    chart: { type: 'line', title: 'Mean lever presses per session', xLabel: 'Day', yLabel: 'Lever presses', categories: ['1', '2', '3', '4', '5'], yMax: 80, yStep: 20, series: [{ name: 'Group 1 (dopamine blocker)', values: [8, 10, 11, 12, 12] }, { name: 'Group 2 (salt solution)', values: [9, 22, 38, 55, 68] }] },
  },
  'e3-kofi': { html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 9–12.</em></p><p>Kofi is preparing for his Year 12 Chemistry exam. Last month he failed a Chemistry SAC. He tells a friend, "I've already lost marks I can't get back." Each evening he plans to study, but he ends up watching videos until late. The morning of the exam, as he walks into the exam hall, his heart races and his mouth goes dry.</p>` },
  'e3-mice': {
    html: `<p><em>Use the following information to answer Questions 13–15.</em></p><p>Researchers gave one group of young mice antibiotics in their drinking water for two weeks, which greatly reduced their gut microbiota. A control group received plain water. After two weeks, all mice learnt a maze, and their memory of the maze was tested the next day by recording how many wrong turns they made.</p>
<table><tr><th>Group</th><th>Mean wrong turns (next day)</th></tr><tr><td>Antibiotics</td><td>11.4</td></tr><tr><td>Plain water (control)</td><td>4.8</td></tr></table>`,
  },
  'e3-synapse': { html: `<p>At one synapse in the hippocampus, Neuron A releases glutamate, which binds to receptor sites on Neuron B. At a nearby synapse, Neuron C releases GABA, which also binds to receptor sites on Neuron B.</p>` },
  'e3-mateo': { html: `<p>Mateo, 19, is an apprentice chef. Three months ago he moved out of home into a share house, and he now works long split shifts with a demanding head chef while struggling to pay rent.</p><ul><li>Weeks 1–6: he felt "wired", kept going on little sleep and coped with the workload.</li><li>Weeks 7–12: he constantly feels exhausted, has had two bouts of flu and has burnt himself twice through careless mistakes.</li></ul><p>Mateo usually copes by sitting down and making a budget, but when the head chef criticises him in front of others, he goes out to the lane to slow his breathing and then refocuses on his station.</p>` },
  'e3-obs': {
    html: `<p>Researchers investigated observational learning in 45 children aged 5–6, with written parental consent. Children were randomly allocated to watch one of three short videos in which an adult tidied up a playroom:</p>
<ul><li>Video A: the adult is praised and given a sticker afterwards</li><li>Video B: the adult is told off for being slow</li><li>Video C: no consequence is shown</li></ul>
<p>Each child was then left alone for five minutes in a messy playroom, and the number of toys they put away was recorded. Children were told they were "helping test some new toys".</p>
<table><tr><th>Video</th><th>Mean toys put away</th></tr><tr><td>A (model rewarded)</td><td>11.2</td></tr><tr><td>B (model punished)</td><td>3.1</td></tr><tr><td>C (no consequence)</td><td>6.5</td></tr></table>`,
  },
  'e3-aph': {
    html: `<p>Researchers asked 30 adults with aphantasia and 30 adults with typical mental imagery to (1) describe a recent birthday, (2) describe how they imagine a holiday next year, and (3) list facts about their own life (e.g. schools attended). Responses were scored for the number of specific details.</p>`,
    chart: { type: 'bar', title: 'Mean number of details', xLabel: 'Task', yLabel: 'Number of details', categories: ['Past event (birthday)', 'Imagined future (holiday)', 'Facts about own life'], yMax: 30, yStep: 5, series: [{ name: 'Aphantasia', values: [12, 9, 18] }, { name: 'Typical imagery', values: [24, 20, 19] }] },
  },
  'e3-zac': { html: `<p>Zac, 17, can't fall asleep until about 1:30 am, even when he goes to bed at 10 pm. On school days he must wake at 6:45 am and feels groggy until lunchtime. On holidays he sleeps from 1:30 am to about 10 am and wakes feeling refreshed. Zac games online in bed most nights, keeps his room warm, and drinks an energy drink after school to "get through homework".</p>` },
  'e3-grace': { html: `<p>Grace, 34, needs an MRI scan, which involves lying still inside a narrow tube for 30 minutes. She has a specific phobia of enclosed spaces that began when she was locked in a dark storeroom for an hour as a child. She has avoided lifts, small rooms and crowded trains for years, and each time she leaves an enclosed space she feels a rush of relief. Grace says, "I remember that storeroom being completely airless. I couldn't breathe at all," although her parents say there was a large air vent. She believes that in the scanner she will "suffocate and nobody will notice."</p>` },
  'e3-lang': { html: `<p>In a regional Victorian town, local Aboriginal Elders designed and run a language revitalisation program for young people. Elders decide what is taught and how. Young people learn language through songs, stories and visits to significant sites on Country, and they teach younger children at the local primary school. Participants describe feeling "stronger in who I am" and more connected to their families and community.</p>` },
  'e3-er': {
    html: `<p>Harper, 70, decided to learn the ukulele by watching online tutorials by a popular teacher whose videos have thousands of grateful comments. She watches each lesson several times, practises daily, and records how many smooth chord changes she can make in one minute.</p>
<p>Her friend says: <em>"At our age the brain can't learn new tricks. You're wasting your time."</em></p>`,
    chart: { type: 'line', title: "Harper's chord changes per minute", xLabel: 'Week', yLabel: 'Chord changes per minute', categories: ['1', '2', '3', '4', '5', '6', '7', '8'], yMax: 40, yStep: 10, series: [{ name: 'Chord changes', values: [6, 9, 13, 16, 21, 24, 27, 30] }] },
  },
});

const E3 = (n) => `e3-a${String(n).padStart(2, '0')}`;

VCE.questions('psychology', [
  // ============================================================ SECTION A
  { id: E3(1), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-div'], stimulus: 'e3-swim',
    stem: '<p>The goosebumps on Ruby\'s arms are best described as</p>',
    options: ['an unconscious response controlled by the autonomic nervous system.', 'a conscious response controlled by the somatic nervous system.', 'a spinal reflex involving an interneuron.', 'a conscious response controlled by the parasympathetic nervous system.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Can Ruby choose to have goosebumps?',
    why: ['Goosebumps occur automatically, without awareness or choice, and are regulated by the autonomic (sympathetic) nervous system.', 'Ruby didn\'t choose to have goosebumps, so they are not conscious.', 'Goosebumps are an autonomic response, not a protective spinal reflex withdrawing from harm.', 'Goosebumps are unconscious, and they involve sympathetic, not parasympathetic, activity.'] },
  { id: E3(2), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-div'], stimulus: 'e3-swim',
    stem: '<p>Which of the following correctly traces Ruby\'s decision to swim back to shore?</p>',
    options: ['temperature receptors → sensory neurons → brain → motor neurons → skeletal muscles', 'temperature receptors → motor neurons → spinal cord → sensory neurons → skeletal muscles', 'brain → sensory neurons → temperature receptors → skeletal muscles', 'temperature receptors → interneuron → motor neurons → skeletal muscles, without the brain'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'A conscious decision needs the brain. Follow the message from receptor to muscle.',
    why: ['Sensory neurons carry the cold information to the CNS; the brain processes it and makes the conscious decision; motor neurons of the somatic nervous system then activate her skeletal muscles to swim.', 'Motor neurons carry messages away from the CNS, not from receptors.', 'Sensory neurons carry messages towards the brain, not away from it.', 'This describes a spinal reflex; Ruby made a deliberate decision, which involves the brain.'] },
  { id: E3(3), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-div'],
    stem: '<p>Which statement about the central nervous system is correct?</p>',
    options: ['It consists of the brain and spinal cord, and the spinal cord carries messages between the brain and the peripheral nervous system.', 'It consists of the brain only.', 'It includes the somatic and autonomic nervous systems.', 'It only produces unconscious responses.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Two structures, and one of them is a "highway".',
    why: ['The CNS is the brain and spinal cord; the spinal cord relays messages between the brain and the PNS and can initiate spinal reflexes.', 'The spinal cord is also part of the CNS.', 'The somatic and autonomic divisions are part of the peripheral nervous system.', 'The brain produces conscious responses.'] },
  { id: E3(4), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-nt', 'le-beh'], stimulus: 'e3-rats',
    stem: '<p>The results suggest that dopamine</p>',
    options: ['plays a key role in reward-based learning.', 'is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter.', 'is required for classical conditioning only.', 'has no effect on behaviour.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Which group learnt to press the lever more and more?',
    why: ['When dopamine receptors were blocked, lever pressing barely increased (8 → 12), whereas the salt-solution group increased sharply (9 → 68). This suggests dopamine is involved in learning from rewards.', 'The main inhibitory neurotransmitter is GABA; dopamine is a neuromodulator.', 'Lever pressing for food is operant conditioning, not classical conditioning.', 'Blocking dopamine clearly changed behaviour.'] },
  { id: E3(5), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-beh'], stimulus: 'e3-rats',
    stem: '<p>In Group 2, the sugar pellet is best described as</p>',
    options: ['positive reinforcement, which increased lever pressing.', 'negative reinforcement, which increased lever pressing.', 'an unconditioned stimulus in classical conditioning.', 'positive punishment, which decreased lever pressing.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Something pleasant is added after a voluntary behaviour.',
    why: ['A pleasant stimulus (sugar) is added after the voluntary behaviour (pressing), and pressing increases. That is positive reinforcement.', 'Nothing unpleasant is removed.', 'The lever press is voluntary and controlled by its consequence, which is operant, not classical, conditioning.', 'Pressing increased, so it was not punished.'] },
  { id: E3(6), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-design', 'sk-method'], stimulus: 'e3-rats',
    stem: '<p>Why was Group 2 injected with a salt solution rather than receiving no injection?</p>',
    options: ['So that both groups experienced the injection, making the dopamine blocker the only difference between them.', 'To increase the number of lever presses in Group 2.', 'To create a second independent variable.', 'Because salt solution blocks dopamine receptors.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'What might the injection itself do to a rat\'s behaviour?',
    why: ['Being injected could itself affect behaviour (e.g. stress), so giving both groups an injection controls this extraneous variable; only the drug differs.', 'The salt solution is chosen because it has no effect.', 'The IV is the presence of the dopamine blocker; the injection is held constant.', 'The salt solution has no effect on dopamine receptors.'] },
  { id: E3(7), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-nt'],
    stem: '<p>If GABA activity in a brain region is too low while glutamate activity is normal, the most likely result is</p>',
    options: ['neurons in that region becoming over-excitable.', 'neurons in that region becoming less likely to fire.', 'no change, because glutamate and GABA act independently.', 'the region releasing more melatonin.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'GABA puts on the brakes.',
    why: ['GABA normally inhibits neural firing and balances glutamate\'s excitation. With too little GABA, excitation goes unchecked and neurons become over-excitable, which is linked to anxiety.', 'Reduced inhibition makes firing more, not less, likely.', 'The balance between excitation and inhibition matters.', 'Melatonin is released by the pineal gland and is not linked to GABA in this way.'] },
  { id: E3(8), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-plast'],
    stem: '<p>As Jasmine becomes an expert touch-typist, she stops looking at the keyboard. Connections in the pathway she used to look for keys are weakened and eventually eliminated. This elimination is called</p>',
    options: ['pruning, associated with long-term depression.', 'sprouting, associated with long-term potentiation.', 'rerouting, associated only with brain injury.', 'potentiation, associated with GABA.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Unused connections are removed.',
    why: ['Repeated low-level use weakens synapses (LTD), and unused connections are eventually removed (pruning).', 'Sprouting creates new connections.', 'Rerouting forms alternative connections; it isn\'t limited to injury and doesn\'t describe elimination.', 'Potentiation means strengthening.'] },
  { id: E3(9), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-tmsc'], stimulus: 'e3-kofi',
    stem: '<p>Kofi\'s statement about his failed SAC, "I\'ve already lost marks I can\'t get back," is best described as a</p>',
    options: ['primary appraisal of harm/loss.', 'primary appraisal of threat.', 'primary appraisal of challenge.', 'secondary appraisal of his coping resources.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Has the damage already happened, or might it happen?',
    why: ['Kofi is judging that damage has already occurred (lost marks), which is a harm/loss appraisal within primary appraisal.', 'Threat concerns possible future harm.', 'Challenge involves seeing potential for growth.', 'He is judging the significance of what happened, not his coping resources.'] },
  { id: E3(10), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-coping'], stimulus: 'e3-kofi',
    stem: '<p>Watching videos instead of studying each evening is best described as</p>',
    options: ['an avoidance strategy.', 'an approach strategy.', 'coping flexibility.', 'a secondary appraisal.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Is Kofi confronting or evading the stressor?',
    why: ['Watching videos distracts Kofi from the stressor (the exam) rather than dealing with it, which is avoidance.', 'An approach strategy would deal with the stressor directly, e.g. studying.', 'Coping flexibility involves switching from an ineffective strategy to a better one.', 'Appraisal is an evaluation, not a coping behaviour.'] },
  { id: E3(11), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-gas', 'st-resp'], stimulus: 'e3-kofi',
    stem: '<p>As Kofi walks into the exam hall, his heart races and his mouth goes dry. According to the General Adaptation Syndrome, this is most consistent with</p>',
    options: ['the counter shock phase of the alarm reaction, when resistance rises above normal.', 'the shock phase, when resistance is above normal.', 'the exhaustion stage, when resistance is below normal.', 'the resistance stage, when the parasympathetic nervous system dominates.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'The sympathetic nervous system has just kicked in.',
    why: ['In counter shock the sympathetic nervous system activates (racing heart, dry mouth from reduced saliva) and resistance rises above normal.', 'In shock, resistance drops below normal.', 'Exhaustion follows prolonged stress and involves depleted resources.', 'Resistance involves sustained cortisol-driven arousal, not parasympathetic dominance.'] },
  { id: E3(12), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-gas'],
    stem: '<p>A strength of the explanatory power of Selye\'s General Adaptation Syndrome is that it</p>',
    options: ['explains how prolonged exposure to stressors can lead to illness.', 'accounts for individual differences in how stressors are appraised.', 'focuses on cognitive processes in the stress response.', 'proposes that every stressor produces a different physiological response.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'GAS is a biological model. What does it do well?',
    why: ['GAS links prolonged stress to the exhaustion stage, providing a biological explanation of why chronic stress can lead to illness.', 'Ignoring individual appraisal is a limitation of GAS.', 'GAS focuses on physiological, not cognitive, processes.', 'GAS proposes the same general response to all stressors.'] },
  { id: E3(13), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-gba', 'sk-conclude'], stimulus: 'e3-mice',
    stem: '<p>Which conclusion is best supported by these results?</p>',
    options: ['Reducing gut microbiota with antibiotics was associated with poorer memory of the maze in these mice.', 'Gut microbiota have no effect on memory.', 'Antibiotics improve memory in humans.', 'The antibiotics caused poorer memory by reducing serotonin production.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Stick to what was measured, in whom.',
    why: ['Mice given antibiotics made more wrong turns (11.4 vs 4.8), indicating poorer memory of the maze after their microbiota were reduced, in this sample of mice.', 'There was a clear difference between groups.', 'The study used mice, and memory was poorer, not better.', 'Serotonin was not measured, so this mechanism goes beyond the data.'] },
  { id: E3(14), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-design'], stimulus: 'e3-mice',
    stem: '<p>The purpose of the plain-water group was to</p>',
    options: ['provide a baseline for comparison, so any difference could be attributed to the antibiotics.', 'act as the dependent variable.', 'increase the sample size of the antibiotic group.', 'control for the number of wrong turns.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Control groups provide…',
    why: ['A control group is not exposed to the IV, providing a baseline against which the experimental group can be compared.', 'The DV is the number of wrong turns.', 'The control group is a separate group, not extra antibiotic mice.', 'Wrong turns are what is being measured.'] },
  { id: E3(15), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-gba'], stimulus: 'e3-mice',
    stem: '<p>The gut–brain axis research shown here is described as "emerging" mainly because</p>',
    options: ['much of the evidence comes from animal studies, and the mechanisms in humans are not yet fully understood.', 'scientists have already proven exactly how gut bacteria control human memory.', 'the gut and the brain are not connected.', 'no research has been conducted in this area.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'What makes a field "emerging"?',
    why: ['Gut–brain axis research is new and rapidly changing; much evidence comes from animals or correlational studies, so cause and effect in humans is still being established.', 'Nothing in this field has been "proven", and current understanding is still developing.', 'The gut and brain are connected via the vagus nerve and the autonomic nervous system.', 'There is a growing body of research.'] },
  { id: E3(16), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-beh'],
    stem: '<p>An energy-drink advertisement repeatedly shows the drink just before scenes of thrilling extreme sports set to exciting music, which naturally make viewers feel excited. The advertisers hope that eventually</p>',
    options: ['the drink itself will become a conditioned stimulus that produces excitement.', 'the exciting scenes will become a neutral stimulus.', 'viewers will be positively punished for buying the drink.', 'the drink will become an unconditioned stimulus.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Classical conditioning: which stimulus starts neutral?',
    why: ['The drink (initially neutral) is paired with the exciting scenes (UCS), so it may become a CS that produces a conditioned excitement response.', 'The exciting scenes are the UCS; they already produce excitement.', 'Punishment would discourage buying.', 'A UCS produces a response naturally, without learning; the drink can only become a conditioned stimulus.'] },
  { id: E3(17), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-beh'],
    stem: '<p>After Leo comes home two hours past his curfew, his parents take away his phone for the weekend. Leo is home on time after that. This is an example of</p>',
    options: ['negative punishment.', 'positive punishment.', 'negative reinforcement.', 'positive reinforcement.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Something pleasant is removed and the behaviour (coming home late) decreases.',
    why: ['A desirable stimulus (his phone) is removed after the behaviour, decreasing the likelihood of coming home late. That is negative punishment.', 'Positive punishment adds something unpleasant.', 'Reinforcement increases a behaviour; coming home late decreased.', 'Reinforcement increases a behaviour; coming home late decreased.'] },
  { id: E3(18), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-obs'],
    stem: '<p>Mila watched a skateboarder\'s trick online. A week later, she could still describe each step of the trick to her friend before trying it. This mainly demonstrates which stage of observational learning?</p>',
    options: ['retention', 'attention', 'reproduction', 'reinforcement'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'She has stored a mental representation.',
    why: ['Being able to describe the steps a week later shows she stored a mental representation of the behaviour in memory, which is retention.', 'Attention occurred when she watched, but describing it a week later shows retention.', 'She has not yet performed the trick.', 'No consequence is described.'] },
  { id: E3(19), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-ab'],
    stem: '<p>Which statement is <strong>not</strong> consistent with Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander ways of knowing as described in the study design?</p>',
    options: ['Knowledge is separated from Country and learnt in isolated, individual lessons.', 'Learning is embedded in relationships.', 'Knowledge is shared through multiple modes such as story, song and dance.', 'The learner is part of a system of knowledge patterned on Country.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Look for the statement that contradicts a systems-based approach.',
    why: ['Separating knowledge from Country and learning in isolation contradicts the relational, Country-patterned nature of Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander ways of knowing.', 'This is consistent: learning is embedded in relationships.', 'This is consistent: knowledge is multimodal.', 'This is consistent: the learner is situated within a system patterned on Country.'] },
  { id: E3(20), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-msm'],
    stem: '<p>According to the Atkinson–Shiffrin multi-store model, retrieval is the process of</p>',
    options: ['recovering information from long-term memory into short-term memory.', 'transferring information from sensory memory to short-term memory.', 'repeating information to keep it in short-term memory.', 'grouping information into larger units.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Bringing a stored memory back into awareness.',
    why: ['Retrieval brings stored information from LTM back into STM, where we become consciously aware of it.', 'This is the result of attention.', 'This is rehearsal.', 'This is chunking.'] },
  { id: E3(21), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-msm'],
    stem: '<p>Which of the following correctly matches each memory store with its approximate duration?</p>', header: ['Iconic memory', 'Short-term memory'],
    options: [['0.2–0.4 seconds', '18–30 seconds'], ['3–4 seconds', '18–30 seconds'], ['0.2–0.4 seconds', 'potentially unlimited'], ['18–30 seconds', '0.2–0.4 seconds']], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Iconic is the shortest of all.',
    why: ['Iconic (visual sensory) memory lasts about 0.2–0.4 seconds and STM about 18–30 seconds without rehearsal.', '3–4 seconds is echoic memory.', 'STM duration is limited; unlimited duration is LTM.', 'The values are reversed.'] },
  { id: E3(22), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-brain'],
    stem: '<p>Years later, Tom vividly remembers exactly where he was and who he was with when he heard that his grandfather had died. The vividness of this explicit memory is best explained by</p>',
    options: ['the amygdala interacting with the hippocampus to strengthen encoding of an emotional event.', 'the cerebellum storing the event as a procedural memory.', 'the basal ganglia forming a habit.', 'rehearsal in sensory memory.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Emotion and memory.',
    why: ['The amygdala adds emotional significance and interacts with the hippocampus during encoding, so emotionally significant events are encoded strongly as vivid explicit memories.', 'This is an explicit episodic memory, not a procedural one.', 'It is not a habit.', 'Sensory memory lasts only seconds.'] },
  { id: E3(23), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-brain'],
    stem: '<p>In a laboratory, a tone was repeatedly followed by a puff of air to the eye until participants blinked at the tone alone. People with damage to which brain region would have the most difficulty acquiring this conditioned eye-blink?</p>',
    options: ['cerebellum', 'neocortex', 'hippocampus', 'amygdala'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'A simple, precisely timed motor reflex.',
    why: ['The cerebellum is involved in implicit memories for simple classically conditioned motor reflexes such as the eye-blink, including their precise timing.', 'The neocortex stores explicit memories.', 'The hippocampus is mainly involved in explicit memories.', 'The amygdala is involved in conditioned emotional (fear) responses, not motor reflexes.'] },
  { id: E3(24), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-brain'],
    stem: '<p>Knowledge that Canberra is the capital of Australia is a</p>',
    options: ['semantic memory stored in the neocortex.', 'episodic memory stored in the cerebellum.', 'procedural memory stored in the basal ganglia.', 'semantic memory stored in the amygdala.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'A general fact, consciously recalled.',
    why: ['General facts are explicit semantic memories, stored long-term in the neocortex.', 'This is a general fact, not a personal event, and the cerebellum stores implicit motor memories.', 'This is not a skill.', 'The amygdala adds emotional significance; it is not the storage site for semantic facts.'] },
  { id: E3(25), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-epi'],
    stem: '<p>Compared with people with typical mental imagery, people with aphantasia are most likely to</p>',
    options: ['recall general facts about their life as well as others, but report fewer sensory details of past events.', 'be unable to recall any facts about their life.', 'show neurofibrillary tangles in the hippocampus.', 'be unable to form any long-term memories.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Aphantasia mainly affects the visual "re-experiencing" of memories.',
    why: ['Semantic memory is typically intact in aphantasia, but without voluntary visual imagery, episodic recall and imagined futures tend to contain fewer sensory details.', 'Semantic memory is typically intact.', 'Tangles are a feature of Alzheimer\'s disease.', 'People with aphantasia form long-term memories normally.'] },
  { id: E3(26), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-mnem'],
    stem: '<p>Which feature of Aboriginal peoples\' songlines most directly helps the <em>retrieval</em> of information?</p>',
    options: ['Features of Country along a journey act as cues for the associated songs and knowledge.', 'Information is written down in a fixed order.', 'Each person creates their own private songline.', 'Songlines rely only on visual imagery.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'What triggers recall when travelling?',
    why: ['Places on Country are linked to songs and stories, so travelling through them provides retrieval cues that trigger the associated knowledge in sequence.', 'Songlines are oral traditions, not written records.', 'Songlines are shared communally across generations.', 'Songlines are multimodal: song, story, dance and place.'] },
  { id: E3(27), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'sleep', kk: ['sl-asc'],
    stem: '<p>Sleep is considered an altered state of consciousness rather than normal waking consciousness mainly because during sleep</p>',
    options: ['awareness of the external environment is greatly reduced and brain wave patterns differ.', 'the brain stops all electrical activity.', 'a person cannot be woken by any stimulus.', 'it is induced by the use of substances.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Think about awareness and brain waves.',
    why: ['Sleep involves reduced awareness of the environment and changed perception and brain wave patterns compared with normal waking consciousness.', 'The brain remains active; in REM it is very active.', 'People can be woken, some stages more easily than others.', 'Sleep is a naturally occurring altered state.'] },
  { id: E3(28), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'sleep', kk: ['sl-asc'],
    stem: '<p>Bursts of brain wave activity called sleep spindles are characteristic of</p>',
    options: ['NREM Stage 2.', 'NREM Stage 3.', 'REM sleep.', 'normal waking consciousness.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'This stage also features K-complexes.',
    why: ['Sleep spindles (and K-complexes) are characteristic of NREM Stage 2.', 'NREM Stage 3 is dominated by high-amplitude, low-frequency waves.', 'REM shows waking-like, high-frequency, low-amplitude waves.', 'Spindles are a feature of sleep.'] },
  { id: E3(29), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'sleep', kk: ['sl-reg'],
    stem: '<p>An adult sleeps for about 7.5 hours. Approximately how many complete NREM–REM cycles would they typically pass through?</p>',
    options: ['1', '2', '5', '10'], answer: 2, fixedOrder: true,
    hint: 'One cycle ≈ 90 minutes.',
    why: ['One cycle lasts about 90 minutes, not the whole night.', 'Two cycles would take only about 3 hours.', 'With cycles of about 90 minutes, 7.5 hours (450 minutes) allows about 5 cycles.', 'Ten cycles would take about 15 hours.'] },
  { id: E3(30), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'sleep', kk: ['sl-reg'],
    stem: '<p>As darkness falls, which sequence correctly describes how a person becomes sleepy?</p>',
    options: ['less light detected by the retina → SCN sends excitatory messages to the pineal gland → melatonin released into the bloodstream', 'less light detected → pineal gland signals the SCN → SCN releases melatonin', 'less light detected → SCN sends inhibitory messages to the pineal gland → melatonin release increases', 'less light detected → adrenal glands release melatonin'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Darkness: the SCN excites the pineal gland.',
    why: ['When little light is detected, the SCN sends excitatory messages to the pineal gland, which releases melatonin into the bloodstream, promoting sleepiness.', 'The SCN signals the pineal gland, and the pineal gland, not the SCN, releases melatonin.', 'Inhibitory messages occur in light and reduce melatonin release.', 'Melatonin comes from the pineal gland.'] },
  { id: E3(31), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'sleep', kk: ['sl-life'],
    stem: '<p>Which explanation best accounts for children having much more NREM Stage 3 sleep than older adults?</p>',
    options: ['NREM Stage 3 supports physical growth and restoration, which children need more of.', 'NREM Stage 3 is when dreaming occurs, which children need more of.', 'Children have more REM sleep, which converts to NREM Stage 3.', 'Older adults have more melatonin.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'What happens in the body during deep sleep?',
    why: ['NREM Stage 3 is associated with physical growth and restoration (e.g. growth hormone release), which is greatest in childhood.', 'Vivid dreaming is associated with REM sleep.', 'Sleep stages don\'t convert into one another.', 'This doesn\'t explain the difference in deep sleep, and older adults typically produce less melatonin.'] },
  { id: E3(32), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'sleepwb', kk: ['sw-crsd'],
    stem: '<p>An older man with Advanced Sleep Phase Disorder falls asleep at 7 pm and wakes at 3 am. The most appropriate bright light therapy is</p>',
    options: ['bright light in the early evening to delay his circadian rhythm.', 'bright light at 3 am to help him stay awake.', 'bright light first thing in the morning.', 'avoiding all light during the day.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'His rhythm needs to move later.',
    why: ['Evening light suppresses melatonin and shifts his circadian rhythm later, so he falls asleep and wakes later.', 'Light at 3 am may shift the rhythm in an unhelpful direction and doesn\'t address his early sleepiness.', 'Morning light would shift his rhythm even earlier.', 'Avoiding daylight would disrupt his circadian rhythm.'] },
  { id: E3(33), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'sleepwb', kk: ['sw-dep'],
    stem: '<p>Sophie sleeps nine hours a night but wakes many times because of noise from a nearby highway. She is most likely experiencing</p>',
    options: ['partial sleep deprivation, due to poor sleep quality.', 'no sleep deprivation, because she sleeps nine hours.', 'full sleep deprivation.', 'Delayed Sleep Phase Syndrome.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Partial sleep deprivation can be about quality, not just quantity.',
    why: ['Partial sleep deprivation includes inadequate sleep quality; frequent waking disrupts her sleep cycles even though total time is adequate.', 'Quality matters as well as quantity.', 'Full sleep deprivation means no sleep at all.', 'Nothing suggests her sleep timing is delayed.'] },
  { id: E3(34), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'wellbeing', kk: ['wb-def'],
    stem: '<p>Which statement best describes resilience?</p>',
    options: ['the ability to cope with and manage change and uncertainty', 'never experiencing stress', 'avoiding situations that might cause difficulty', 'having a high level of functioning at all times'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'The study design definition.',
    why: ['The study design defines resilience as the ability to cope with and manage change and uncertainty.', 'Resilient people still experience stress; they cope with it.', 'Avoidance is the opposite of managing change.', 'Functioning fluctuates even in resilient people.'] },
  { id: E3(35), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'wellbeing', kk: ['wb-cont'],
    stem: '<p>Two friends both fail their driving test. Amara is disappointed for a day and books another; Liv feels hopeless for weeks and stops going out. The difference between them is best explained by</p>',
    options: ['the interaction of internal factors (e.g. thoughts, resilience) with the same external factor.', 'the external factor being different for each friend.', 'a fixed position on the mental wellbeing continuum.', 'Liv having a specific phobia of driving.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Same stressor, different responses.',
    why: ['They faced the same external factor (failing), so the differences in their responses reflect internal factors such as their thoughts, resilience and coping, which interact with the external event.', 'The external factor was the same.', 'Mental wellbeing fluctuates; it isn\'t fixed.', 'Nothing indicates an irrational fear of driving.'] },
  { id: E3(36), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'maintain', kk: ['mt-cult'],
    stem: '<p>Young people learning their language and ceremonies from Elders is the clearest example of which cultural determinant?</p>',
    options: ['cultural continuity', 'self-determination', 'levels of functioning', 'resilience'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Passing culture across generations.',
    why: ['Passing language and ceremony from Elders to young people maintains culture across generations, which is cultural continuity.', 'Self-determination is about communities making decisions for themselves.', 'Levels of functioning is a way of considering mental wellbeing, not a cultural determinant.', 'Resilience is a way of considering mental wellbeing.'] },
  { id: E3(37), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'phobia', kk: ['ph-dev'],
    stem: '<p>Precipitation of a specific phobia by classical conditioning occurs when</p>',
    options: ['a previously neutral stimulus is paired with a frightening experience and comes to trigger fear.', 'avoiding the feared stimulus reduces anxiety.', 'a person imagines the worst possible outcome.', 'friends tease a person about their fear.'], answer: 0,
    hint: '"Precipitation" = how it starts.',
    why: ['In classical conditioning, a neutral stimulus is associated with a frightening UCS and becomes a CS that elicits fear, which is how a phobia can be precipitated.', 'This describes perpetuation by operant conditioning (negative reinforcement).', 'This is catastrophic thinking.', 'This is a social factor.'] },
  { id: E3(38), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'phobia', kk: ['ph-dev'],
    stem: '<p>Before a flight, Nick thinks, "If there\'s turbulence the plane will break apart and we\'ll all die." This is an example of</p>',
    options: ['catastrophic thinking.', 'memory bias.', 'negative reinforcement.', 'GABA dysfunction.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'A prediction of the worst possible outcome.',
    why: ['Predicting the worst possible outcome from a situation is catastrophic thinking.', 'Memory bias is distorted recall of past events.', 'Negative reinforcement is about removing something unpleasant after a behaviour.', 'GABA dysfunction is a biological factor, not a thought.'] },
  { id: E3(39), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'phobia', kk: ['ph-int'],
    stem: '<p>Which of the following is the correct order of steps in systematic desensitisation?</p>',
    options: ['learn relaxation → construct a fear hierarchy → gradually work up the hierarchy while relaxed', 'construct a fear hierarchy → face the most feared situation first → learn relaxation', 'take benzodiazepines → avoid the stimulus → learn relaxation', 'challenge thoughts → avoid the stimulus → construct a fear hierarchy'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Relaxation must be learnt before exposure begins.',
    why: ['Systematic desensitisation involves learning relaxation, building a fear hierarchy, and then gradually working through it from least to most feared while relaxed.', 'Exposure starts at the bottom of the hierarchy, and relaxation is learnt first.', 'Avoidance and medication are not steps of systematic desensitisation.', 'Avoidance is not part of the treatment.'] },
  { id: E3(40), pool: 'exam3', type: 'mc', marks: 1, topic: 'maintain', kk: ['mt-bps'],
    stem: '<p>Mindfulness meditation acts as a psychological protective factor mainly because it involves</p>',
    options: ['focusing attention on the present moment and accepting thoughts and feelings without judgement.', 'eliminating all negative thoughts permanently.', 'avoiding thinking about stressors by keeping busy.', 'increasing GABA levels through medication.'], answer: 0,
    hint: 'Two components: attention and acceptance.',
    why: ['Mindfulness meditation combines attention to the present (e.g. the breath) with non-judgemental acceptance of thoughts and feelings, reducing rumination and stress.', 'Mindfulness doesn\'t eliminate thoughts; it changes the relationship to them.', 'Keeping busy to avoid thinking is an avoidance strategy.', 'Mindfulness is a psychological technique, not a medication.'] },

  // ============================================================ SECTION B
  // Q1 – neural transmission (8)
  { id: 'e3-b1a', pool: 'exam3', label: '1a', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-nt'], stimulus: 'e3-synapse',
    stem: '<p>Describe how glutamate is transmitted from Neuron A to Neuron B. Refer to the parts of the neurons involved.</p>', hint: 'Vesicles → axon terminal → synaptic gap → receptor sites on dendrites.',
    criteria: ['Glutamate is released from synaptic vesicles in the axon terminals of Neuron A (pre-synaptic) into the synaptic gap', 'It crosses the gap and binds to complementary receptor sites on the dendrites of Neuron B (post-synaptic)'],
    model: '<p>Glutamate is stored in synaptic vesicles in the axon terminals of Neuron A and released into the synaptic gap. It crosses the gap and binds to receptor sites on the dendrites of Neuron B whose shape is complementary to glutamate.</p>', pitfalls: ['Mixing up axon terminals and dendrites.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b1b', pool: 'exam3', label: '1b', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-nt'], stimulus: 'e3-synapse',
    stem: '<p>Explain the different effects glutamate and GABA would have on Neuron B, and why a balance between them is important.</p>', hint: 'Excitatory vs inhibitory, then what happens if one dominates.',
    criteria: ['Glutamate has an excitatory effect: it makes Neuron B more likely to fire', 'GABA has an inhibitory effect: it makes Neuron B less likely to fire', 'Balance is important so neural activity is regulated, e.g. too little GABA inhibition could lead to over-excitation (linked to anxiety), while glutamate excitation is needed for learning'],
    model: '<p>Glutamate is excitatory: when it binds to Neuron B\'s receptors, it makes Neuron B more likely to fire. GABA is inhibitory: it makes Neuron B less likely to fire. A balance is important because the overall activity of Neuron B depends on both. Without enough GABA inhibition, neurons can become over-excited, which is linked to anxiety, while glutamate\'s excitation is needed for processes such as learning and long-term potentiation.</p>', pitfalls: ['Stating effects without the "why balance matters" part.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b1c', pool: 'exam3', label: '1c', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'ns', kk: ['ns-nt', 'st-gba'],
    stem: '<p>Explain how serotonin differs from GABA in the way it affects brain activity, and explain one way serotonin links to the gut–brain axis.</p>', hint: 'Neuromodulator vs neurotransmitter, then where most serotonin is.',
    criteria: ['Serotonin is a neuromodulator: it acts on a widespread group of neurons, altering their responsiveness (slower, longer-lasting)', 'GABA is a neurotransmitter: it acts at a specific synapse with a direct inhibitory effect on the post-synaptic neuron', 'GBA link: about 90% of the body\'s serotonin is found in the gut and gut microbiota can produce/influence it, communicating with the brain via the gut–brain axis (vagus nerve), influencing mood'],
    model: '<p>Serotonin is a neuromodulator: it acts on many neurons across widespread brain areas, changing how responsive they are to other neurotransmitters, with slower and longer-lasting effects on mood and the sleep-wake cycle. GABA is a neurotransmitter that acts at a specific synapse, directly making the post-synaptic neuron less likely to fire. Serotonin links to the gut–brain axis because about 90% of the body\'s serotonin is found in the gut, and gut microbiota can influence its production, which may affect mood via communication along the vagus nerve.</p>', pitfalls: ['Saying serotonin crosses directly from gut to brain. Keep it to the gut–brain axis communication.'] },
  // Q2 – stress (9)
  { id: 'e3-b2a', pool: 'exam3', label: '2a', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-gas', 'st-resp'], stimulus: 'e3-mateo',
    stem: '<p>Using Selye\'s General Adaptation Syndrome, identify the stage Mateo was most likely in during weeks 1–6 and during weeks 7–12. Justify each using evidence from the scenario.</p>', hint: 'Coping vs depleted.',
    criteria: ['Weeks 1–6: resistance stage, with resistance above normal and cortisol keeping him going ("wired", coping on little sleep)', 'Weeks 7–12: exhaustion stage, as his resources are depleted and resistance falls below normal', 'Evidence for exhaustion: constant exhaustion, two bouts of flu (immune suppression from prolonged cortisol), careless mistakes'],
    model: '<p>In weeks 1–6, Mateo was most likely in the resistance stage: cortisol kept his arousal and resistance above normal, so he felt "wired" and coped with the workload despite little sleep. In weeks 7–12 he had moved into the exhaustion stage: his resources were depleted and resistance fell below normal, shown by constant exhaustion, two bouts of flu (prolonged cortisol suppressing his immune system) and careless mistakes that led to burns.</p>', pitfalls: ['Calling weeks 1–6 the alarm reaction. That lasts only a short time.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b2b', pool: 'exam3', label: '2b', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-coping'], stimulus: 'e3-mateo',
    stem: '<p>Explain how Mateo demonstrates coping flexibility.</p>', hint: 'Different strategy for a different stressor.',
    criteria: ['Mateo uses an approach strategy (making a budget) for financial stress', 'For a different stressor (public criticism, which he can\'t fix on the spot), he switches to a different strategy suited to it (slowing his breathing, then refocusing), showing he can adjust strategies to suit the situation'],
    model: '<p>Mateo uses budgeting, an approach strategy, to deal with rent stress. But when he is criticised in front of others during service, budgeting would not help, so he switches to slowing his breathing in the lane and then refocusing on his station. Adjusting his strategy to suit the demands of the situation shows coping flexibility.</p>', pitfalls: ['Only defining the term.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b2c', pool: 'exam3', label: '2c', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-tmsc'], stimulus: 'e3-mateo',
    stem: '<p>Using the Transactional Model of Stress and Coping, explain why another apprentice in the same kitchen might experience much less stress than Mateo.</p>', hint: 'Different appraisals of the same situation.',
    criteria: ['Stress depends on the individual\'s appraisal: the other apprentice may appraise the demanding kitchen as a challenge or benign rather than a threat (primary appraisal)', 'They may judge that they have adequate coping resources, e.g. no rent pressure, family support or more experience (secondary appraisal), so demands don\'t exceed resources'],
    model: '<p>According to the model, stress depends on each person\'s appraisal. Another apprentice might appraise the demanding head chef as a challenge to improve rather than a threat (primary appraisal). They might also judge that they have enough coping resources, such as living at home without rent or having more experience (secondary appraisal), so they experience less stress.</p>', pitfalls: ['Only referring to one type of appraisal.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b2d', pool: 'exam3', label: '2d', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'stress', kk: ['st-gas', 'st-tmsc'],
    stem: '<p>Give one limitation of Selye\'s General Adaptation Syndrome that the Transactional Model of Stress and Coping addresses.</p>', hint: 'What does GAS ignore?',
    criteria: ['GAS assumes everyone responds with the same physiological pattern to any stressor and ignores psychological factors/individual differences', 'The TMSC addresses this by explaining that stress depends on each person\'s cognitive appraisal of the stressor and their coping resources'],
    model: '<p>GAS assumes everyone shows the same physiological stress response to any stressor and does not consider psychological factors. The Transactional Model addresses this by proposing that stress depends on how each individual appraises the stressor and their resources, which explains individual differences.</p>', pitfalls: ['A limitation that TMSC does not address.'] },
  // Q3 – observational learning research (10)
  { id: 'e3-b3a', pool: 'exam3', label: '3a', type: 'sa', marks: 1, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-design'], stimulus: 'e3-obs',
    stem: '<p>Identify the dependent variable.</p>', hint: 'What was measured?',
    criteria: ['The number of toys each child put away'], model: '<p>The number of toys each child put away in five minutes.</p>', pitfalls: ['Naming the video condition (that is the IV).'] },
  { id: 'e3-b3b', pool: 'exam3', label: '3b', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-method'], stimulus: 'e3-obs',
    stem: '<p>Name the experimental design used and explain why random allocation was important.</p>', hint: 'One video per child.',
    criteria: ['Between-subjects design (each child watched only one video)', 'Random allocation spreads participant characteristics (e.g. natural tidiness) evenly across groups, so differences are more likely due to the video, increasing internal validity'],
    model: '<p>A between-subjects design was used, because each child watched only one of the three videos. Random allocation was important because it spreads differences between children, such as how tidy they usually are, evenly across the groups, so any difference in toys put away is more likely to be caused by the video, increasing internal validity.</p>', pitfalls: ['Confusing random allocation with random sampling.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b3c', pool: 'exam3', label: '3c', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-obs'], stimulus: 'e3-obs',
    stem: '<p>Explain the results, referring to the data and to the stages of observational learning.</p>', hint: 'All groups attended and retained. What differed between them?',
    criteria: ['Data: rewarded model 11.2 > no consequence 6.5 > punished model 3.1', 'All children attended to and retained the tidying behaviour, and could reproduce it', 'The consequence for the model affected motivation via vicarious reinforcement (increasing tidying) or vicarious punishment (decreasing it)'],
    model: '<p>Children who saw the model rewarded put away the most toys (11.2), followed by those who saw no consequence (6.5), with the fewest in the punished-model group (3.1). All children paid attention to the video, retained the behaviour and were able to reproduce it, so the differences reflect motivation. Seeing the model praised (vicarious reinforcement) increased children\'s motivation to tidy, while seeing the model told off reduced it.</p>', pitfalls: ['Not quoting the data.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b3d', pool: 'exam3', label: '3d', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-ethics'], stimulus: 'e3-obs',
    stem: '<p>Children were told they were "helping test some new toys". Name the ethical guideline this involves and explain what the researchers must do as a result.</p>', hint: 'They weren\'t told the real purpose.',
    criteria: ['Use of deception (justified because knowing the aim might change their tidying behaviour)', 'Debriefing: at the end, children (and parents) should be told the true purpose in an age-appropriate way, questions answered and support provided'],
    model: '<p>The study used deception, because the children were not told the real aim; knowing it might have changed how much they tidied. As a result, the researchers must debrief the children and their parents afterwards, explaining the true purpose in age-appropriate language, answering questions and making sure no child leaves upset.</p>', pitfalls: ['Only naming informed consent.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b3e', pool: 'exam3', label: '3e', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-conclude'], stimulus: 'e3-obs',
    stem: '<p>Explain one limitation that affects the extent to which these findings could be applied to everyday life.</p>', hint: 'Sample? Setting? Time?',
    criteria: ['Limitation, e.g. children aged only 5–6 / small sample (15 per group) / a short video and a single five-minute test in an artificial setting', 'Effect: findings may not generalise to older children or adults, or to real homes where behaviour is shaped over longer periods (limited external validity)'],
    model: '<p>The children were tested only once, for five minutes, straight after a short video in an unfamiliar playroom. Tidying at home is shaped by long-term family routines, so the findings may not generalise to everyday behaviour, which limits external validity.</p>', pitfalls: ['Limitation without effect.'] },
  // Q4 – aphantasia research (10)
  { id: 'e3-b4a', pool: 'exam3', label: '4a', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-epi', 'sk-data'], stimulus: 'e3-aph',
    stem: '<p>Describe the results for each of the three tasks.</p>', hint: 'Compare the two groups task by task, with numbers.',
    criteria: ['Past event: aphantasia group recalled fewer details (12) than the typical imagery group (24)', 'Imagined future: aphantasia group produced fewer details (9) than the typical imagery group (20)', 'Facts about own life: both groups were similar (18 vs 19)'],
    model: '<p>For the past event, people with aphantasia recalled about half as many details as the typical imagery group (12 vs 24). For the imagined future, they also produced fewer details (9 vs 20). For facts about their own life, both groups performed similarly (18 vs 19).</p>', pitfalls: ['Describing only differences, ignoring the semantic task.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b4b', pool: 'exam3', label: '4b', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-epi'], stimulus: 'e3-aph',
    stem: '<p>Explain these results with reference to episodic memory, semantic memory and visual imagery.</p>', hint: 'Which tasks rely on re-experiencing with imagery, and which on facts?',
    criteria: ['Recalling the birthday relies on episodic memory, which involves re-experiencing sensory details, often through visual imagery; without voluntary imagery, fewer details are retrieved', 'Imagining the future involves recombining episodic (and semantic) details into a mental scene, which also relies on imagery, so the aphantasia group produced fewer details', 'Facts about one\'s life are semantic memories that don\'t depend on visual imagery, so the groups were similar'],
    model: '<p>Recalling a birthday draws on episodic memory, which involves mentally re-experiencing sensory details, often through visual imagery. Because people with aphantasia lack voluntary visual imagery, they retrieved fewer details. Imagining a future holiday involves recombining episodic and semantic details into a mental scene, which also relies heavily on imagery, so again they produced fewer details. Facts about one\'s own life are semantic memories that don\'t require visual imagery, so both groups performed similarly.</p>', pitfalls: ['Implying aphantasia damages memory itself.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b4c', pool: 'exam3', label: '4c', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'memory', kk: ['me-brain'],
    stem: '<p>Explain the roles of the hippocampus and the neocortex when a person retrieves facts about their own life.</p>', hint: 'Storage vs interaction during retrieval.',
    criteria: ['Neocortex: the long-term storage site of explicit semantic memories (facts about one\'s life)', 'Hippocampus: interacts with the neocortex to retrieve explicit memories (and consolidated them originally)'],
    model: '<p>Facts about one\'s own life are explicit semantic memories stored in the neocortex. The hippocampus, which originally consolidated these memories, interacts with the neocortex during retrieval to bring them back into conscious awareness.</p>', pitfalls: ['Saying the hippocampus is the long-term store.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b4d', pool: 'exam3', label: '4d', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'skills', kk: ['sk-conclude', 'sk-data'], stimulus: 'e3-aph',
    stem: '<p>Explain why the researchers should be cautious about concluding that aphantasia causes the differences in detail.</p>', hint: 'Participants weren\'t allocated to "aphantasia". What else could differ?',
    criteria: ['Groups were pre-existing (not randomly allocated), so the IV wasn\'t manipulated and other differences between groups could explain the results', 'E.g. verbal ability, age or motivation may differ between groups (extraneous variables); scoring "details" from self-report may also introduce error'],
    model: '<p>Participants weren\'t randomly allocated to have aphantasia; the groups already existed, so they may differ in other ways, such as age, verbal ability or how much detail they tend to give. These extraneous variables could explain the differences, so the researchers cannot be certain that aphantasia itself caused them.</p>', pitfalls: ['Only saying "small sample".'] },
  // Q5 – sleep (11)
  { id: 'e3-b5a', pool: 'exam3', label: '5a', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'sleepwb', kk: ['sw-crsd'], stimulus: 'e3-zac',
    stem: '<p>Identify the circadian rhythm sleep disorder Zac most likely has and justify your answer.</p>', hint: 'His sleep is fine in length but shifted.',
    criteria: ['Delayed Sleep Phase Syndrome (DSPS)', 'His sleep onset is consistently delayed (~1:30 am) by more than 2 hours, but when allowed to sleep on his own schedule (holidays) he sleeps a normal amount and wakes refreshed'],
    model: '<p>Zac most likely has Delayed Sleep Phase Syndrome. He consistently can\'t fall asleep until about 1:30 am, well over two hours later than intended, yet on holidays, when he can follow his own schedule, he sleeps a normal amount and wakes refreshed. His sleep is shifted later rather than poor in itself.</p>', pitfalls: ['Calling it insomnia.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b5b', pool: 'exam3', label: '5b', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'sleepwb', kk: ['sw-crsd', 'sl-reg'], stimulus: 'e3-zac',
    stem: '<p>Explain how bright light therapy could be used to treat Zac\'s disorder. Refer to the suprachiasmatic nucleus and melatonin.</p>', hint: 'Timing, mechanism, outcome.',
    criteria: ['Exposure to bright light soon after waking in the morning (e.g. a light box for about 30 minutes)', 'Light detected by the retina signals the SCN, which inhibits melatonin release from the pineal gland in the morning', 'Repeated morning exposure shifts (advances) his circadian rhythm earlier, so melatonin is released earlier at night and he falls asleep earlier'],
    model: '<p>Zac would sit in front of a bright light box for about 30 minutes soon after waking each morning. The light is detected by his retina and signalled to his SCN, which inhibits the pineal gland\'s release of melatonin, helping him feel alert. Repeated morning exposure shifts his circadian rhythm earlier, so melatonin is released earlier in the evening and he can fall asleep earlier.</p>', pitfalls: ['Evening light (which would delay him further).'] },
  { id: 'e3-b5c', pool: 'exam3', label: '5c', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'sleepwb', kk: ['sw-hyg'], stimulus: 'e3-zac',
    stem: '<p>Recommend three changes to Zac\'s sleep hygiene based on the scenario, and justify each.</p>', hint: 'Blue light, temperature, drinking patterns.',
    criteria: ['Stop gaming in bed / avoid screens about an hour before bed, because blue light suppresses melatonin release via the SCN', 'Keep his bedroom cool, because a drop in core body temperature helps sleep onset', 'Avoid energy drinks after school, because caffeine is a stimulant that delays sleep'],
    model: '<p>First, Zac should stop gaming in bed and avoid screens for about an hour before sleep, because blue light inhibits melatonin release and delays sleep onset. Second, he should keep his bedroom cool rather than warm, because a drop in core body temperature helps him fall asleep. Third, he should avoid energy drinks after school, because caffeine is a stimulant that can keep him alert well into the night.</p>', pitfalls: ['Generic advice not drawn from the scenario.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b5d', pool: 'exam3', label: '5d', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'sleepwb', kk: ['sw-dep'], stimulus: 'e3-zac',
    stem: '<p>Zac regularly gets about 5 hours of sleep on school nights. Describe one affective, one behavioural and one cognitive effect this could have on him at school.</p>', hint: 'One of each, applied to school.',
    criteria: ['Affective: e.g. irritability/moodiness with friends or teachers', 'Behavioural: e.g. yawning, microsleeps or putting his head down in morning classes', 'Cognitive: e.g. poor concentration or memory in morning classes/tests'],
    model: '<p>Affective: Zac may be irritable and short-tempered with friends and teachers. Behavioural: he may yawn constantly or have microsleeps in morning classes. Cognitive: he may struggle to concentrate and remember content, especially in morning lessons when he feels groggy.</p>', pitfalls: ['Mislabelling concentration as behavioural.'] },
  // Q6 – specific phobia (12)
  { id: 'e3-b6a', pool: 'exam3', label: '6a', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'phobia', kk: ['ph-dev'], stimulus: 'e3-grace',
    stem: '<p>Identify the cognitive bias shown when Grace describes the storeroom, and explain how it contributes to her phobia.</p>', hint: 'Compare her memory with her parents\' account.',
    criteria: ['Memory bias: she recalls the storeroom as "completely airless" although there was a large vent', 'This distorted, fear-consistent memory exaggerates the danger of enclosed spaces, increasing her anxiety and maintaining the phobia'],
    model: '<p>Grace shows memory bias: she remembers the storeroom as "completely airless" although her parents say it had a large air vent. This distorted memory exaggerates how dangerous enclosed spaces are, which increases her anxiety about them and helps maintain her phobia.</p>', pitfalls: ['Choosing catastrophic thinking for the storeroom memory (that applies to "I\'ll suffocate").'] },
  { id: 'e3-b6b', pool: 'exam3', label: '6b', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'phobia', kk: ['ph-dev'], stimulus: 'e3-grace',
    stem: '<p>Explain how operant conditioning has perpetuated Grace\'s phobia.</p>', hint: 'What happens when she leaves an enclosed space?',
    criteria: ['Leaving/avoiding enclosed spaces removes her anxiety (rush of relief): negative reinforcement', 'This strengthens avoidance, so she never learns that enclosed spaces are safe, which maintains the phobia'],
    model: '<p>Each time Grace avoids or leaves an enclosed space, her anxiety is removed and she feels relief. This negative reinforcement strengthens her avoidance behaviour, so she keeps avoiding lifts and small rooms and never learns that they are safe, which perpetuates her phobia.</p>', pitfalls: ['Calling relief positive reinforcement.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b6c', pool: 'exam3', label: '6c', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'phobia', kk: ['ph-int'], stimulus: 'e3-grace',
    stem: '<p>Explain how cognitive behavioural therapy could help Grace, referring to her specific thoughts and behaviours.</p>', hint: 'Cognitive: "I\'ll suffocate". Behavioural: avoidance.',
    criteria: ['Cognitive: identify her catastrophic thought ("I\'ll suffocate and nobody will notice")', 'Challenge and replace it with realistic thoughts (e.g. MRI scanners are well ventilated, staff can see and hear her, she can press a call button)', 'Behavioural: gradual exposure to enclosed spaces (e.g. lifts, small rooms) instead of avoiding them, reducing anxiety and breaking the negative reinforcement of avoidance'],
    model: '<p>In CBT, Grace\'s psychologist would help her identify her catastrophic thought that she will "suffocate and nobody will notice" in the scanner. Together they would challenge it with evidence (MRI scanners are ventilated, staff can see and hear her, and she can use a call button) and replace it with a realistic thought. In the behavioural component, Grace would gradually expose herself to enclosed spaces such as lifts and small rooms rather than avoiding them, so she learns they are safe and her anxiety decreases.</p>', pitfalls: ['Only one component.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b6d', pool: 'exam3', label: '6d', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'phobia', kk: ['ph-int'], stimulus: 'e3-grace',
    stem: '<p>Explain how breathing retraining could help Grace during the scan.</p>', hint: 'Physiological arousal and control.',
    criteria: ['Slow, controlled (diaphragmatic) breathing counteracts the rapid breathing/hyperventilation of the anxiety response', 'This reduces physiological arousal (sympathetic activity) and the feeling that she can\'t breathe, giving her a sense of control while lying still'],
    model: '<p>Breathing retraining teaches Grace to use slow, controlled diaphragmatic breathing. During the scan this would counteract rapid, shallow breathing, reducing her physiological arousal and the sensation that she can\'t breathe, and giving her a sense of control while she lies still.</p>', pitfalls: ['Describing it as a psychological intervention.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b6e', pool: 'exam3', label: '6e', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'phobia', kk: ['ph-int'], stimulus: 'e3-grace',
    stem: '<p>Grace\'s doctor offers a short-acting benzodiazepine before the scan. Evaluate this option for Grace.</p>', hint: 'How does it work, why it suits this situation, and one limitation.',
    criteria: ['Mechanism: a GABA agonist that enhances GABA\'s inhibitory effect, reducing neural firing and anxiety', 'Strength in this context: fast, short-term relief for a single, necessary 30-minute procedure, making it more likely she can complete the scan', 'Limitation: it doesn\'t treat the cause of the phobia (avoidance and catastrophic thinking remain), with possible side effects (drowsiness) and dependence if used repeatedly, so it should be combined with psychological treatment'],
    model: '<p>A benzodiazepine is a GABA agonist: it enhances GABA\'s inhibitory effect, reducing neural firing and Grace\'s physiological anxiety. This is a sensible short-term option because the scan is a single, necessary 30-minute procedure, and the medication makes it more likely she can lie still and complete it. However, it only manages symptoms: it doesn\'t change her catastrophic thinking or avoidance, so her phobia will remain. It can also cause drowsiness and dependence if used repeatedly. It is appropriate for this scan, but should be combined with a psychological treatment such as CBT for long-term improvement.</p>', pitfalls: ['No judgement.'] },
  // Q7 – wellbeing (10)
  { id: 'e3-b7a', pool: 'exam3', label: '7a', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'maintain', kk: ['mt-cult'], stimulus: 'e3-lang',
    stem: '<p>Explain how the program reflects self-determination.</p>', hint: 'Who makes the decisions?',
    criteria: ['Local Aboriginal Elders design and run the program', 'They decide what is taught and how, i.e. the community makes decisions about matters affecting its own young people'],
    model: '<p>The program is designed and run by local Aboriginal Elders, who decide what is taught and how. The community is therefore making decisions about matters that affect its own young people, which is self-determination.</p>', pitfalls: ['Describing cultural continuity instead.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b7b', pool: 'exam3', label: '7b', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'maintain', kk: ['mt-cult', 'wb-def'], stimulus: 'e3-lang',
    stem: '<p>Explain how the program supports cultural continuity and why this could strengthen participants\' social and emotional wellbeing.</p>', hint: 'Passing on culture → domains of SEWB.',
    criteria: ['Cultural continuity: language, songs and stories are passed from Elders to young people and on to younger children, maintaining culture across generations', 'Strengthens connection to SEWB domains such as culture, Country (visits to significant sites), family and kinship, and community', 'Evidence: participants feel "stronger in who I am" and more connected, showing stronger identity and SEWB'],
    model: '<p>The program supports cultural continuity because language, songs and stories are passed from Elders to young people, who then pass them on to younger children, keeping culture alive across generations. This strengthens young people\'s connections to SEWB domains such as culture, Country (through visits to significant sites), family and kinship, and community. Participants\' comments that they feel "stronger in who I am" and more connected show how this supports their holistic social and emotional wellbeing.</p>', pitfalls: ['Not linking to SEWB domains.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b7c', pool: 'exam3', label: '7c', type: 'sa', marks: 2, topic: 'wellbeing', kk: ['wb-def'],
    stem: '<p>Explain why social and emotional wellbeing (SEWB) is described as a multidimensional framework.</p>', hint: 'Many domains.',
    criteria: ['SEWB includes many interconnected domains/elements of being: body; mind and emotions; family and kinship; community; culture; Country; spirituality and ancestors', 'Wellbeing depends on the quality of connections across all of these domains, not one dimension (such as mental health) alone'],
    model: '<p>SEWB is multidimensional because it considers wellbeing across many interconnected domains: connection to body, mind and emotions, family and kinship, community, culture, Country, and spirituality and ancestors. A person\'s wellbeing reflects the quality of their connections across all of these, rather than a single dimension such as mental health.</p>', pitfalls: ['Listing only two domains.'] },
  { id: 'e3-b7d', pool: 'exam3', label: '7d', type: 'sa', marks: 3, topic: 'maintain', kk: ['mt-bps'],
    stem: '<p>Year 12 student Ari has stopped seeing friends, skips breakfast and lies awake worrying. Explain how one biological, one psychological and one social protective factor could help maintain Ari\'s mental wellbeing.</p>', hint: 'One from each domain, linked to Ari.',
    criteria: ['Biological: e.g. regular nutrition (eating breakfast) and hydration, or adequate sleep, supporting energy, mood and concentration', 'Psychological: e.g. mindfulness meditation (attention to the present, acceptance) or cognitive behavioural strategies to reduce night-time worry', 'Social: reconnecting with friends who provide authentic and energising support'],
    model: '<p>Biologically, eating breakfast and staying hydrated would give Ari more energy and steadier mood and concentration. Psychologically, practising mindfulness meditation before bed (focusing on the breath and accepting worries without judgement) could reduce rumination and help him sleep. Socially, reconnecting with friends who offer authentic and energising support would give him a sense of belonging and help buffer stress.</p>', pitfalls: ['Generic factors not linked to Ari.'] },
  // Q8 – extended response (10)
  { id: 'e3-b8', pool: 'exam3', label: '8', type: 'er', marks: 10, topic: 'learning', kk: ['le-obs', 'ns-plast', 'sk-data'], stimulus: 'e3-er',
    stem: '<p>Evaluate Harper\'s friend\'s claim that "at our age the brain can\'t learn new tricks". In your response, refer to observational learning, synaptic plasticity and Harper\'s data.</p>',
    hint: 'Observational learning stages applied to Harper; LTP/LTD and sprouting/pruning; the trend in her data; limits of one person\'s self-recorded data; a judgement.',
    criteria: [
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Attention:</strong> Harper watches each lesson several times, focusing on the teacher' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Retention:</strong> she stores mental representations of the chord shapes and changes' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Reproduction:</strong> daily practice develops the physical ability to perform the chord changes' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Motivation/reinforcement:</strong> vicarious reinforcement (thousands of grateful comments) motivates her; her own improvement provides direct reinforcement' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Synaptic plasticity:</strong> repeated practice → LTP strengthens synapses in the pathway for chord changes (sprouting)' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>LTD/pruning:</strong> LTD and pruning remove inefficient movements; together they refine the pathway (plasticity continues throughout life)' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Data:</strong> chord changes rose steadily from 6 per minute (week 1) to 30 (week 8), a fivefold improvement' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Connection:</strong> the steady improvement is consistent with ongoing synaptic changes from practice' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Limitations:</strong> one person, self-recorded data (possible error/bias), no comparison with younger learners, so not generalisable' },
      { m: 1, t: '<strong>Judgement:</strong> the claim is not supported. Harper\'s case and the mechanism of plasticity indicate older adults can learn new skills (perhaps more slowly), though stronger evidence would need larger samples' },
    ],
    model: `<p>Harper's learning shows observational learning, a social-cognitive approach. She pays attention by watching each lesson several times and retains mental representations of the chord shapes and changes. Through daily practice she develops the physical ability to reproduce the movements. She is motivated by vicarious reinforcement, having seen thousands of grateful comments from other learners, and her own steady progress provides direct reinforcement.</p>
<p>At a neural level, each time Harper practises a chord change, the pathway involved is repeatedly stimulated, and long-term potentiation strengthens those synapses, with sprouting forming new connections. Meanwhile, long-term depression weakens and prunes connections for clumsy or inefficient finger movements. Together these processes refine the pathway so the movements become smoother and more automatic. Synaptic plasticity is the fundamental mechanism of learning, and it continues throughout life.</p>
<p>Harper's data support this. Her chord changes per minute rose steadily from 6 in week 1 to 30 in week 8, a fivefold improvement with no sign of levelling off, consistent with ongoing synaptic change. However, this is one person's self-recorded data, which may contain measurement error or bias, and there is no comparison with younger learners, so it cannot show whether older adults learn at the same rate. The results can't be generalised to all older people.</p>
<p>Overall, the friend's claim is not supported. Harper's progress, explained by observational learning and the brain's lifelong synaptic plasticity, shows that older adults can learn new skills, even if perhaps more slowly than younger people. Stronger conclusions would require research with larger samples of older adults.</p>`,
    pitfalls: ['Describing observational learning without applying each stage to Harper.', 'Ignoring the graph or not quoting values.', 'No final judgement on the claim.', 'Describing LTP without LTD (or vice versa).'],
  },
]);

